> In article <1993Feb3.210215.110@noose.ecn.purdue.edu> kavuri@lips2.ecn.purdue.edu (Surya N Kavuri ) writes:
> >  
> >  When I leave a level with some corpses of dead creatures
> >  and return after a long time, the display does not clear
> >  the corpses until I reach the spot.
> >
> 
> That's a feature. How can you know if the corpse is still there if you can't
> see it ? So it only shows you the new state when you get ot it !!

I like this behavior... it's more fully consistent than before...
my only complaint is that it's sometimes a nuisance to have several
copies of a symbol on the screen when you know very well only the one
in the room with you is real.  E.g. if your dog keeps following you and
dropping something, then picking it up when you leave the room.

But I can't see a good fix for this.
Just suppose it were feasible, when you see an object, to check all
over the level for old images of that object, which you would now know
to be no longer true.  You could erase them all -- but what symbol would
you replace them with?  To retain consistency with what you know, you
would have to replace them with the symbol of what was under them
_when_you_last_saw_them_.  Not what's there now.  And if *that* symbol
had to be erased because the object there was brought by a monster into
your view, then the next item from when you last saw that square has
to be drawn.  That means that for every square you've seen, a extra stack
of symbols has to be kept around!  A stack whose length has no reasonable
bound.  Granted this stack would be null for most squares...
But anyway it doesn't seem worth it.

And I'd rather keep the current consistency than have some half-solution.

Well, maybe not...

A half-solution would be, whenever erasing an "invalidated" screen symbol,
you just put the symbol for that square there, e.g. empty space, altar,
passageway, whatever.  So it would show an empty space when the space
really wasn't empty.  Then if you went back there it would be updated
correctly.

Nah, this would mean that if you were looking for a pile, you'd have to
go back to every empty-looking room just to check.
Well, you kind of do anyway, since a monster might have carried it there
and dropped it.

Sorry for rambling.
My conclusion is, forget it unless you can think of a better way to
go about it.

